    "acquired_at": "2026-11-20T23:45:28.238998+00:00",
    "available_at": "2026-11-20T23:45:28.238998+00:00",
    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Honda Africa Twin Gets Biggest Refresh in a Decade, They Now Call It CRF1150L\nAudi Will Build a New-Generation A8 After All. This Is Why It Might Not Make It to the U.S.\n14 photos\nPhoto: Audi\nAudi pulled the plug on the A8 with no successor in sight. However, it turns out that the Ingolstadt-based automaker is, indeed, working on a flagship sedan to replace the D5 generation, just not for America. The German firm is negotiating the use of a Chinese platform, a move that would lock the luxury sedan out of the U.S.\nAudi stopped taking orders for the A8 in Germany in February 2026. Production of the sedan ended at the Neckarsulm factory in Germany in mid-October 2026, after a 32-year run . The German automaker never said anything about a successor. Until now.\nNow, the automaker brings back hope to those who were planning to buy a new generation of the A8. The all-time rival of the Mercedes-Benz S-Class and BMW 7 Series is making a comeback before 2030. The automaker confirmed to Auto Bild España that a next-generation model will be rolled out with a hybrid powertrain.\nHowever, Audi might have to keep the A8 out of America. And that is because the carmaker is considering using a Chinese platform for the upcoming model. While the model will retain the internal combustion engine, almost certainly running on gas, it will be paired with an electric motor rather than fully transitioning to an EV-only lineup.\nPhoto: Audi However, in a conversation with Auto Bild España, the automaker's representatives declined to confirm the ongoing rumors concerning a partnership with Huawei for a potential jointly developed architecture. The architecture is built for pure electric or range-extended electric (EREV), tailored to Chinese luxury buyer preferences. In the meantime, both Europe and the U.S. markets still demand hybrids.\nA Huawei-developed platform built in China cannot be sold in the U.S. due to connected-vehicle software/hardware bans, issued by the U.S. Department of Commerce's Bureau of Industry and Security. The architecture should go into production at the FAW-Audi joint-venture plant in Changchun to utilize spare capacity.\nThe platform will not be imported to the U.S. and might not comply with European standards, which means that Audi might have to develop a bespoke platform solely for Europe and the U.S., which sounds more like a money pit than a profitable strategy, considering the collapsing popularity of sedans.\nLast year, Audi sold only 1,406 A8s in the United States, which was 13.6% down from 2024. In the meantime, BMW moved 11,393 7 Series examples (including the i7), and Mercedes-Benz delivered 7,408 units last year.\nPhoto: Audi Considering the circumstances, Audi might resort to the MSB architecture, which underpins the Porsche Panamera . The MSB is developed for front-engine, rear- or all-wheel-drive luxury vehicles and is capable of supporting both combustion and hybrid powertrains.\nIt is a race against time for Audi to find a way for the A8 to go into production before the end of the decade. With the final D5 generation model rolling off the assembly line in October and the next-gen sedan not arriving until late in the decade, Audi will have a multi-year gap without a flagship sedan in its portfolio.\nDuring this interval, the upcoming three-row Audi Q9 SUV will serve as the brand's flagship model, sitting at the top of the lineup. The Q9 will be built in Perry, Georgia, at the Middle Georgia Mega Site near Macon. Last month, Audi announced plans for a $4 billion investment and a second-phase $3 million expansion of its first-ever manufacturing center in the United States.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nAudi A8 Audi A8 next-generation Audi audi usa Audi Huawei\nAbout the author: Elena Luchian\nElena started off as a sports reporter and five years later switched to automotive. How does that happen? Lots of writing, testdrives, traveling, off-road driving courses, track driving courses, defensive driving courses and diplomas. The diplomas might be \"useless\", but the knowledge she gained is priceless. Full profile\nUP NEXT Dear Petrolheads, Get Ready for the First-Ever BMW M3 Electric – Rendering",
